// Sentinel incidents at their LATEST state, bucketed by age - what is actually open, how old it
// is, and how much has been closed. The answer to "do we have a years-old backlog".
//
// Loaded by Search-MsecLogAnalytics -Subject Sentinel -Name IncidentBacklog -WorkspaceName <ws>.
//
// SECURITYINCIDENT IS A CHANGE LOG, NOT AN INCIDENT LIST, and reading it without arg_max is the
// mistake this file exists to prevent. Every status change, comment and reassignment writes a
// new row, so a plain count answers "how many times did incidents change" - a number that looks
// like a backlog and is not one. Measured on one workspace: 2,488 rows, 2,394 distinct
// incidents, of which 2,359 were closed and 35 open. Counting rows would have reported the
// backlog as 70x its real size.
//
// WHY NOT ASK DEFENDER XDR INSTEAD. Graph /security/incidents only surfaces recent incidents -
// on the same tenant it returned nothing older than three weeks while this table held 21 months.
// An "is there an old backlog" question answered from Graph alone comes back empty and sounds
// definitive. Get-MsecDefenderIncident -AnyAge is the right call for the live set; this is the
// right one for history.
//
// STATUS IS "New", "Active" or "Closed" - NOT the Defender XDR vocabulary. An incident can sit
// in New indefinitely; New is not a synonym for recent, and the age buckets are what separate
// the two.
SecurityIncident
| summarize arg_max(TimeGenerated, *) by IncidentNumber
| extend AgeDays = datetime_diff('day', now(), CreatedTime)
| extend AgeBucket = case(AgeDays > 730, "> 2 years",
                          AgeDays > 365, "1-2 years",
                          AgeDays > 180, "6-12 months",
                          AgeDays > 90,  "3-6 months",
                          AgeDays > 30,  "1-3 months",
                                         "< 1 month")
| summarize Incidents = count(),
            Oldest    = min(CreatedTime),
            Newest    = max(CreatedTime)
            by Status, AgeBucket, Severity
| order by Status asc, Incidents desc
